<p>Today Tim Cook is stepping down as Apple CEO and Phil Schiller is <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-08-31/apple-s-phil-schiller-steps-down-from-running-app-store-and-product-events">scaling back his involvement</a> in the company’s events and his supervision of the App Store.</p>
<p>I thought I’d find the oldest Tim Cook photo in my Photos library (what, do you not have numerous Apple executives in your People view?) and it’s from October 2008:</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/tim-jobs-phil-6c.jpg?ssl=1" alt="Three men seated on stage in jeans and dark or blue shirts; large Apple logo above on blue backdrop." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption>Tim (left), Phil (right), and some other guy.</figcaption></figure>
<p>This was from the post-event Q&amp;A session, back when that was a thing!</p>
<p>In the <a href="https://youtu.be/jQ7c9CFaHjw?t=54">video of this event</a> you’ll see that Tim Cook, then Apple’s COO, is asked by Steve Jobs to present “the state of the Mac.” If you’re used to Cook being more of an emcee for presentations by others in a pre-baked video, you may be amused to see him working hard to present Apple’s Mac lineup to an in-person crowd of media people. (There’s also a Jony Ive live presentation!)</p>
